WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.200
- Right now in the U.S. labor market,

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.770
we're looking at two numbers.

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:07.840
The first is 106.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.450
That's the number of consecutive months

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.800
the U.S. labor market has added jobs,

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.520
the most ever recorded.
(pleasant orchestral music)

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.840
The second is 3.2%.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.680
That's about how much wages have grown

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.590
each month in 2019 from a year earlier.

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:23.990
In a healthy economy, annual wage growth

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.980
is usually around 3.5%,

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.280
so despite record-breaking jobs growth,

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.820
wages aren't growing as fast as expected

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:34.240
and many economists aren't
sure we're going to see

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:35.808
much of a pickup from here.

00:00:35.808 --> 00:00:40.308
(pleasant piano and orchestral music)

00:00:44.172 --> 00:00:46.250
Let's take a closer look at the numbers.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:49.420
In theory, low unemployment
should encourage employers

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:52.840
to raise wages since they're
competing to retain workers

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:56.010
and also hire from a
shrinking pool of employees,

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.030
but that's not what's happening.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.220
Employee pay and benefits as a percentage

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.730
of gross domestic income fell to 52.7%

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.550
in last year's third quarter,

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.750
marking the fourth
straight quarterly decline.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:09.970
Compare that to when it was

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:14.970
as high as 59% in 1970 and 57% in 2001.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:19.840
In other words, if workers
commanded as much domestic income

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.300
as they did in 2001, they'd
have nearly $800 billion more

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.650
in their pockets or $5100
per employed American.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.890
Economists have identified some
forces contributing to this.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.450
One of the factors squeezing Americans

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.330
out of higher wages is automation.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.900
Rapid advancements in robotics technology

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:41.770
and artificial intelligence
are expected to displace

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.200
millions of jobs in coming decades

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.960
as routine tasks from driving trucks

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.083
to checking out at the grocery
store become automated.

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:54.050
The share of tasks at risk
for food preparation workers,

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:57.650
for example, is 91%.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.590
Economists say workers in industries

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:01.590
where automation is taking over jobs

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.040
have less power to
bargain for higher wages.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.020
Analysts at the Brookings Institution

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:10.210
say less educated workers
are especially likely

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.710
to have their jobs
displaced by automation,

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:14.640
so it ends up being these workers

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.950
who have a harder time
demanding higher wages.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.440
Another contributing factor
to slower wage growth

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.750
is the decrease in unions.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.490
Union membership in 2018

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:27.680
is about half of what it was in 1983.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.870
That means the power to
bargain for higher wages

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:32.130
has decreased, and employment contracts

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.860
have become less favorable for workers.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.410
For example, employment
contracts have become

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.623
increasingly riddled with
noncompete agreements.

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:44.940
Evan Starr, an economist at
the University of Maryland,

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.590
estimates that roughly
one in seven workers

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:51.680
making $40,000 or less signed noncompetes.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.400
His research found that states
that enforce the clauses

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.150
see lower wages across the
course of workers' careers

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.580
than states that don't.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:00.980
That's because noncompetes
can discourage workers

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.260
in low-paying jobs from looking elsewhere

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:04.220
since they might not be able

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.830
to move from one company to another.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:09.100
Even interns are increasingly being hit

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.100
by noncompetes, which can prevent them

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.180
from moving on from temporary jobs

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:16.030
to full-time gigs that
would pay them more.

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.630
Another factor contributing
to lower wage growth

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.550
is the growing gig economy.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:22.630
With the growth of the
freelance and gig economy,

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:24.820
there's more uncertainty for workers.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.960
This gives them less
leverage for negotiation

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.270
and fewer rights than full-time
employees would receive.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.920
In some cases, gig economy
workers have been able

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:34.930
to push for bigger paychecks.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:38.410
New York passed laws in 2018
that required Lyft and Uber

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:42.533
to pay drivers a minimum
of $17.22 an hour,

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:45.820
but that's not been the case everywhere.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.700
Globalization is another reason

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.760
why wage growth has been sluggish.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.980
Because of increased
competition from overseas

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:53.970
where wages are sometimes lower,

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:56.190
American companies are often reluctant

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:59.000
to raise wages for U.S. workers.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.080
U.S. companies have been outsourcing jobs

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.040
to places like Mexico, China, and India

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.770
where they can produce
goods for much cheaper.

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:08.550
That means U.S. workers have been left

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:11.630
with less bargaining power when
it comes to their own wages,

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.990
the idea being if your boss
can find a worker overseas

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:17.330
who's willing to take a
much smaller paycheck,

00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:20.110
why would they be willing to pay you more.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:22.910
For example, Mexican
vehicle assembly workers

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:27.190
made less than $8 a
hour on average in 2017.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:30.370
In comparison, the median wage
for North American workers

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:35.180
in the auto manufacturing
industry was 15 to $16 an hour,

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:38.400
so how do we predict wage growth
will change in the future?

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.910
Is this slower growth
expected to continue?

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.070
It's tough to say how this will play out,

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:46.780
but members of Congress
are pushing for legislation

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:49.700
to increase the minimum
wage, and recently,

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.440
the House did pass the Raise the Wage Act,

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.830
although people say it's probably unlikely

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:56.257
to make it through the Senate.

00:04:56.257 --> 00:04:59.924
(pleasant orchestral music)

